1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,490
Type one question into ChatGPT tonight.

2
00:00:03,030 --> 00:00:04,940
Before the first word comes back,

3
00:00:05,050 --> 00:00:07,280
your question lands in a building full of chips,

4
00:00:07,450 --> 00:00:09,360
all doing math at the same time.

5
00:00:09,950 --> 00:00:10,850
Most of the world's

6
00:00:10,850 --> 00:00:12,740
AI chips come from one company.

7
00:00:13,340 --> 00:00:15,360
A company that started in a Denny's,

8
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:16,890
making chips for video games.

9
00:00:17,610 --> 00:00:19,150
Nvidia isn't an AI company

10
00:00:19,150 --> 00:00:20,720
that used to make gaming chips.

11
00:00:21,290 --> 00:00:22,930
It's a gaming company whose math

12
00:00:22,930 --> 00:00:24,940
turned out to be the math of AI.

13
00:00:25,860 --> 00:00:27,830
And the size of it breaks your brain.

14
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:30,890
When Nvidia went public in 1999,

15
00:00:31,420 --> 00:00:33,690
it raised about 42 million dollars.

16
00:00:34,430 --> 00:00:36,210
Today, it takes in that much money

17
00:00:36,210 --> 00:00:37,540
in less than an hour.

18
00:00:38,390 --> 00:00:40,350
It's worth about 5 trillion dollars,

19
00:00:40,350 --> 00:00:42,240
the most valuable company on Earth.

20
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:44,260
And the man who runs it once cleaned

21
00:00:44,260 --> 00:00:46,330
toilets at a school for troubled kids.

22
00:00:47,470 --> 00:00:49,000
Here's what should be impossible.

23
00:00:49,780 --> 00:00:50,720
In the nineties,

24
00:00:50,720 --> 00:00:51,800
dozens of companies were

25
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:52,970
making graphics chips.

26
00:00:53,560 --> 00:00:55,420
Nvidia's first chip flopped.

27
00:00:55,420 --> 00:00:57,290
It came within weeks of running out of money.

28
00:00:57,840 --> 00:00:58,760
The king of the industry

29
00:00:58,760 --> 00:01:01,250
was a company called 3dfx.

30
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:03,990
Nvidia ended up buying what was left of it.

31
00:01:04,810 --> 00:01:06,150
So how did a chip for

32
00:01:06,150 --> 00:01:08,000
video games end up running AI?

33
00:01:08,790 --> 00:01:09,770
Why did Wall Street hate

34
00:01:09,770 --> 00:01:11,100
the bet that made it rich?

35
00:01:11,990 --> 00:01:14,180
And can anything knock it off the top?

36
00:01:14,840 --> 00:01:17,290
This is the story of a booth at Denny's,

37
00:01:17,340 --> 00:01:18,420
a name a toilet paper

38
00:01:18,420 --> 00:01:19,710
company got to first,

39
00:01:20,040 --> 00:01:21,250
a rescue from Sega,

40
00:01:21,890 --> 00:01:23,030
and two gaming cards

41
00:01:23,030 --> 00:01:25,780
at a student's house that changed computers forever.

42
00:01:26,980 --> 00:01:28,430
The whole history of Nvidia.

43
00:01:32,700 --> 00:01:34,020
It starts in 1973,

44
00:01:34,020 --> 00:01:36,100
when a nine-year-old named Jensen

45
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:37,710
Huang is sent to America.

46
00:01:38,370 --> 00:01:40,530
His relatives enroll him at what they think

47
00:01:40,530 --> 00:01:42,560
is a fancy boarding school in Kentucky.

48
00:01:43,230 --> 00:01:45,140
It's actually a school for troubled kids.

49
00:01:45,370 --> 00:01:46,900
His job is cleaning the toilets.

50
00:01:47,290 --> 00:01:48,190
Every day.

51
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:49,650
His roommate is seventeen,

52
00:01:49,860 --> 00:01:50,970
covered in tattoos,

53
00:01:51,040 --> 00:01:52,050
and can't read.

54
00:01:52,610 --> 00:01:54,280
So Jensen teaches him to read,

55
00:01:54,490 --> 00:01:56,600
and the roommate teaches Jensen to bench press.

56
00:01:57,170 --> 00:01:57,930
At fifteen,

57
00:01:57,930 --> 00:01:59,700
he gets a job at a Denny's in Oregon.

58
00:02:00,350 --> 00:02:02,120
Washing dishes, then bussing tables,

59
00:02:02,250 --> 00:02:03,150
then waiting them.

60
00:02:03,450 --> 00:02:04,590
Remember Denny's.

61
00:02:04,590 --> 00:02:05,490
It comes back.

62
00:02:06,240 --> 00:02:07,730
He studies electrical engineering,

63
00:02:08,220 --> 00:02:09,480
and by his late twenties he's

64
00:02:09,480 --> 00:02:11,430
designing chips in Silicon Valley.

65
00:02:11,880 --> 00:02:13,030
In late 1992,

66
00:02:13,160 --> 00:02:14,650
he sits down with two engineers,

67
00:02:14,840 --> 00:02:16,680
Chris Malachowsky and Curtis Priem,

68
00:02:16,680 --> 00:02:17,910
at a Denny's in San Jose.

69
00:02:18,510 --> 00:02:19,540
Coffee, eggs,

70
00:02:19,790 --> 00:02:20,760
hours of talking.

71
00:02:21,270 --> 00:02:23,780
The plan: a chip that makes video games look real.

72
00:02:24,340 --> 00:02:25,430
In April 1993,

73
00:02:25,860 --> 00:02:28,663
they start the company with about 40 thousand dollars.

74
00:02:28,860 --> 00:02:30,030
They don't have a name yet,

75
00:02:30,060 --> 00:02:31,520
so they label their files NV,

76
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:32,850
for next version.

77
00:02:33,720 --> 00:02:35,580
They want to call it NVision.

78
00:02:35,580 --> 00:02:38,250
But a toilet paper company already has it.

79
00:02:38,700 --> 00:02:39,760
So Jensen picks a word

80
00:02:39,760 --> 00:02:41,090
with an N and a V:

81
00:02:41,240 --> 00:02:43,355
invidia, Latin for envy.

82
00:02:43,560 --> 00:02:45,270
Then he pitches his old boss,

83
00:02:45,460 --> 00:02:47,850
who calls it one of the worst pitches he's ever heard,

84
00:02:48,040 --> 00:02:50,030
and phones a famous investor anyway.

85
00:02:50,770 --> 00:02:51,670
That investor,

86
00:02:51,690 --> 00:02:53,610
Don Valentine of Sequoia,

87
00:02:53,610 --> 00:02:55,380
puts money in, with a warning.

88
00:02:55,790 --> 00:02:56,950
If you lose my money,

89
00:02:56,950 --> 00:02:57,920
I will kill you.

90
00:03:02,360 --> 00:03:03,580
In 1995,

91
00:03:03,580 --> 00:03:05,330
Nvidia's first chip comes out.

92
00:03:05,640 --> 00:03:06,970
It's called NV1,

93
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:09,050
and it builds 3D worlds out of curves.

94
00:03:09,730 --> 00:03:10,960
Everyone else picks triangles.

95
00:03:11,410 --> 00:03:12,330
And when Microsoft builds

96
00:03:12,330 --> 00:03:14,460
its game graphics around triangles too,

97
00:03:14,610 --> 00:03:15,840
the curve bet is dead.

98
00:03:16,320 --> 00:03:17,500
Nvidia is also building

99
00:03:17,500 --> 00:03:19,330
a chip for Sega's next console,

100
00:03:19,400 --> 00:03:20,410
the same wrong way.

101
00:03:20,910 --> 00:03:22,640
So Jensen does something wild.

102
00:03:22,750 --> 00:03:24,210
He tells Sega to find another partner,

103
00:03:24,210 --> 00:03:26,480
and asks them to pay Nvidia anyway.

104
00:03:26,940 --> 00:03:28,040
Sega's American boss,

105
00:03:28,040 --> 00:03:29,850
Shoichiro Irimajiri,

106
00:03:29,920 --> 00:03:32,160
says yes. 5 million dollars.

107
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:34,410
Enough to stay alive for about six months.

108
00:03:34,920 --> 00:03:36,740
Nvidia lays off about half its people

109
00:03:36,740 --> 00:03:39,570
and bets everything on one chip that draws triangles,

110
00:03:39,820 --> 00:03:40,720
the RIVA 128.

111
00:03:41,400 --> 00:03:43,510
It ships in August 1997,

112
00:03:43,720 --> 00:03:45,970
with about one month of payroll left in the bank.

113
00:03:46,420 --> 00:03:47,440
It sells about a million

114
00:03:47,440 --> 00:03:48,970
units in four months.

115
00:03:49,520 --> 00:03:50,780
And one sentence becomes

116
00:03:50,780 --> 00:03:52,530
the company's unofficial motto.

117
00:03:53,140 --> 00:03:54,580
Our company is thirty days

118
00:03:54,580 --> 00:03:55,870
from going out of business.

119
00:04:00,280 --> 00:04:01,480
On January 22nd,

120
00:04:01,480 --> 00:04:02,610
1999,

121
00:04:02,700 --> 00:04:04,950
Nvidia goes public at 12 dollars a share.

122
00:04:05,420 --> 00:04:06,450
Remember that 12 dollars.

123
00:04:07,050 --> 00:04:09,390
That same year it launches the GeForce 256,

124
00:04:09,390 --> 00:04:11,500
and sells it with a brand new word:

125
00:04:11,570 --> 00:04:12,820
the world's first GPU.

126
00:04:13,640 --> 00:04:15,120
So what is a GPU,

127
00:04:15,120 --> 00:04:16,020
actually?

128
00:04:16,370 --> 00:04:19,060
To draw one frame of a game on an HD screen,

129
00:04:19,250 --> 00:04:20,920
you color about two million pixels.

130
00:04:21,510 --> 00:04:22,600
Then you do it again,

131
00:04:22,770 --> 00:04:23,940
sixty times a second.

132
00:04:24,470 --> 00:04:25,500
A normal processor,

133
00:04:25,710 --> 00:04:26,610
a CPU,

134
00:04:26,730 --> 00:04:28,380
is like a few brilliant professors.

135
00:04:28,770 --> 00:04:31,620
They can solve anything, but one problem at a time.

136
00:04:32,100 --> 00:04:33,940
A GPU is a stadium full

137
00:04:33,940 --> 00:04:36,270
of kids who can only do simple sums.

138
00:04:36,520 --> 00:04:38,410
But they all do them at the same moment.

139
00:04:39,320 --> 00:04:41,250
One kid adds a little red to this pixel.

140
00:04:41,480 --> 00:04:42,810
Another darkens that one.

141
00:04:42,840 --> 00:04:43,850
Two million kids,

142
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:45,410
one sum each, done.

143
00:04:45,900 --> 00:04:46,930
For coloring pixels,

144
00:04:47,160 --> 00:04:48,070
the stadium wins.

145
00:04:48,340 --> 00:04:49,240
Every time.

146
00:04:49,880 --> 00:04:53,390
In 2000, Nvidia agrees to buy the remains of 3dfx,

147
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:55,690
the company that used to rule gaming graphics.

148
00:04:56,340 --> 00:04:57,390
Earlier that year,

149
00:04:57,460 --> 00:04:58,880
Microsoft picked Nvidia for

150
00:04:58,880 --> 00:05:00,470
the graphics in its first Xbox,

151
00:05:00,760 --> 00:05:02,990
and paid 200 million dollars up front.

152
00:05:03,760 --> 00:05:05,620
And when the stock hits 100 dollars,

153
00:05:05,620 --> 00:05:06,900
Jensen gets the company logo

154
00:05:06,900 --> 00:05:08,490
tattooed on his body.

155
00:05:12,740 --> 00:05:13,970
Meanwhile, at Stanford,

156
00:05:14,120 --> 00:05:15,380
a grad student named Ian Buck

157
00:05:15,380 --> 00:05:17,150
is wiring GeForce cards together.

158
00:05:17,720 --> 00:05:19,280
He builds a language that lets them do

159
00:05:19,280 --> 00:05:21,190
math that has nothing to do with games.

160
00:05:21,960 --> 00:05:23,810
Nvidia hires him in 2004.

161
00:05:24,340 --> 00:05:27,010
In 2006, Nvidia announces CUDA.

162
00:05:27,360 --> 00:05:29,640
It turns that stadium of kids loose on any problem:

163
00:05:29,640 --> 00:05:30,930
weather, medicine,

164
00:05:31,020 --> 00:05:31,920
physics.

165
00:05:31,950 --> 00:05:33,050
And it builds CUDA into

166
00:05:33,050 --> 00:05:34,620
its ordinary gaming chips.

167
00:05:35,470 --> 00:05:37,070
Millions of gamers carry a supercomputer

168
00:05:37,070 --> 00:05:38,760
feature they will never use.

169
00:05:39,270 --> 00:05:42,660
The idea: put the tool in millions of computers first,

170
00:05:43,050 --> 00:05:45,280
and the people who need it will find it.

171
00:05:45,710 --> 00:05:47,060
Wall Street sees the bill,

172
00:05:47,190 --> 00:05:48,090
not the point.

173
00:05:48,660 --> 00:05:49,620
In 2008,

174
00:05:49,620 --> 00:05:50,880
Nvidia takes a charge of about

175
00:05:50,880 --> 00:05:53,420
200 million dollars for faulty laptop chips,

176
00:05:53,420 --> 00:05:54,560
and the stock falls

177
00:05:54,560 --> 00:05:56,310
31 percent in one day.

178
00:05:56,760 --> 00:05:58,060
By the end of that year,

179
00:05:58,060 --> 00:05:59,140
it has lost about three

180
00:05:59,140 --> 00:06:00,650
quarters of its value.

181
00:06:01,620 --> 00:06:03,110
Remember thirty days from dead?

182
00:06:03,280 --> 00:06:05,810
This time, Nvidia keeps paying for CUDA anyway.

183
00:06:06,310 --> 00:06:07,270
A tool for a market

184
00:06:07,270 --> 00:06:08,840
that doesn't exist yet.

185
00:06:13,160 --> 00:06:14,530
Then, in 2012,

186
00:06:14,760 --> 00:06:16,160
the market finally shows up,

187
00:06:16,160 --> 00:06:17,790
at a student's parents' house.

188
00:06:18,250 --> 00:06:19,440
Alex Krizhevsky,

189
00:06:19,590 --> 00:06:21,610
his friend Ilya Sutskever and their

190
00:06:21,610 --> 00:06:23,630
professor Geoffrey Hinton want to

191
00:06:23,630 --> 00:06:25,480
teach a computer to recognize pictures.

192
00:06:26,140 --> 00:06:27,160
They train a neural network

193
00:06:27,160 --> 00:06:29,090
on two Nvidia gaming cards,

194
00:06:29,200 --> 00:06:30,260
GTX 580s,

195
00:06:30,260 --> 00:06:31,190
for about a week.

196
00:06:31,620 --> 00:06:33,220
It enters the biggest image contest

197
00:06:33,220 --> 00:06:35,390
in the world and wins by a mile:

198
00:06:35,440 --> 00:06:37,010
about 15 percent wrong,

199
00:06:37,080 --> 00:06:39,270
when the next best was 26.

200
00:06:39,500 --> 00:06:40,760
The math inside a neural network

201
00:06:40,760 --> 00:06:43,670
is the same kind of simple sums as coloring pixels.

202
00:06:44,020 --> 00:06:45,830
Millions of them, all at once.

203
00:06:46,420 --> 00:06:48,430
The stadium of kids was built for games.

204
00:06:48,700 --> 00:06:50,050
It was perfect for AI.

205
00:06:50,740 --> 00:06:51,900
You may have heard Nvidia

206
00:06:51,900 --> 00:06:53,410
just got lucky with AI.

207
00:06:53,700 --> 00:06:54,600
Partly true.

208
00:06:54,700 --> 00:06:55,860
But luck only pays if

209
00:06:55,860 --> 00:06:57,270
you're already standing there.

210
00:06:57,900 --> 00:06:59,470
Jensen goes all in.

211
00:06:59,660 --> 00:07:00,570
In 2016,

212
00:07:00,740 --> 00:07:02,633
he hand-delivers the first DGX-1,

213
00:07:02,633 --> 00:07:03,760
an AI computer,

214
00:07:03,760 --> 00:07:05,060
to a small lab

215
00:07:05,060 --> 00:07:07,050
in San Francisco called OpenAI.

216
00:07:07,710 --> 00:07:11,080
He signs it: to the future of computing and humanity.

217
00:07:11,720 --> 00:07:14,120
One of OpenAI's founders is Ilya Sutskever.

218
00:07:14,120 --> 00:07:15,550
From the student's house.

219
00:07:16,050 --> 00:07:18,100
Then crypto miners buy up gaming cards,

220
00:07:18,470 --> 00:07:19,740
crypto crashes in 2018,

221
00:07:20,230 --> 00:07:21,270
and Nvidia's stock loses

222
00:07:21,270 --> 00:07:22,960
more than half its value in months.

223
00:07:23,540 --> 00:07:25,360
Regulators later fine it 5.5 million

224
00:07:25,360 --> 00:07:27,120
dollars for not telling investors how

225
00:07:27,120 --> 00:07:28,730
much crypto was behind its sales.

226
00:07:33,080 --> 00:07:34,160
On November 30th,

227
00:07:34,160 --> 00:07:35,250
2022,

228
00:07:35,260 --> 00:07:37,490
OpenAI releases ChatGPT.

229
00:07:38,140 --> 00:07:40,300
It was trained on a Microsoft supercomputer built

230
00:07:40,300 --> 00:07:42,430
from tens of thousands of Nvidia chips.

231
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:44,240
Suddenly every tech giant

232
00:07:44,240 --> 00:07:46,010
on Earth wants the same thing,

233
00:07:46,260 --> 00:07:48,070
and one company sells most of them.

234
00:07:48,450 --> 00:07:50,520
In a gold rush, sell shovels.

235
00:07:51,110 --> 00:07:52,670
Nvidia's top AI chip,

236
00:07:52,670 --> 00:07:53,570
the H100,

237
00:07:53,850 --> 00:07:56,030
sells for tens of thousands of dollars each,

238
00:07:56,030 --> 00:07:58,200
and buyers wait months to get one.

239
00:07:58,610 --> 00:07:59,960
In May 2023,

240
00:07:59,970 --> 00:08:01,880
Nvidia is worth a trillion dollars.

241
00:08:02,270 --> 00:08:03,640
Denny's puts a plaque on the booth:

242
00:08:03,710 --> 00:08:06,120
the booth that launched a 1 trillion dollar company.

243
00:08:06,560 --> 00:08:07,770
In June 2024,

244
00:08:07,940 --> 00:08:09,700
it passes Microsoft and Apple to become

245
00:08:09,700 --> 00:08:11,730
the most valuable company in the world.

246
00:08:12,210 --> 00:08:13,740
Then, in January 2025,

247
00:08:14,310 --> 00:08:16,030
a Chinese lab called DeepSeek shows

248
00:08:16,030 --> 00:08:17,480
off a cheap AI model,

249
00:08:17,510 --> 00:08:19,950
and Nvidia loses about 589 billion

250
00:08:19,950 --> 00:08:21,620
dollars of value in one day.

251
00:08:22,060 --> 00:08:23,180
The biggest one-day loss

252
00:08:23,180 --> 00:08:24,630
in stock market history.

253
00:08:25,230 --> 00:08:26,540
And then it just keeps climbing.

254
00:08:26,690 --> 00:08:28,560
4 trillion in July 2025.

255
00:08:29,030 --> 00:08:30,940
5 trillion that October.

256
00:08:31,510 --> 00:08:33,720
The first company ever to reach either one.

257
00:08:37,970 --> 00:08:39,660
Which brings us to right now.

258
00:08:39,730 --> 00:08:41,220
October 2026.

259
00:08:41,810 --> 00:08:42,900
In its last quarter,

260
00:08:43,010 --> 00:08:46,770
Nvidia brought in 96.2 billion dollars.

261
00:08:46,770 --> 00:08:49,200
Data centers alone were 89 billion.

262
00:08:49,990 --> 00:08:52,190
That's more than nine of every ten dollars.

263
00:08:52,190 --> 00:08:54,520
The gaming company now mostly sells to AI.

264
00:08:55,150 --> 00:08:56,140
Its newest chips,

265
00:08:56,290 --> 00:08:57,970
Vera Rubin, are in full production,

266
00:08:57,970 --> 00:09:00,690
and it expects 108 billion this quarter,

267
00:09:00,690 --> 00:09:03,380
without counting any AI chip sales to China.

268
00:09:03,930 --> 00:09:05,280
But it's not a straight line.

269
00:09:05,770 --> 00:09:07,980
On October 6th the stock hit a record,

270
00:09:08,170 --> 00:09:09,250
then chip stocks fell eight

271
00:09:09,250 --> 00:09:10,280
days in a row,

272
00:09:10,410 --> 00:09:12,460
their longest streak since 2008.

273
00:09:13,030 --> 00:09:16,480
Today, Nvidia is worth about 5.2 trillion.

274
00:09:17,010 --> 00:09:19,420
And remember that 12 dollar share from 1999?

275
00:09:20,010 --> 00:09:21,240
After six stock splits,

276
00:09:21,270 --> 00:09:22,580
it's 480 shares,

277
00:09:22,750 --> 00:09:25,040
worth about 104 thousand dollars.

278
00:09:26,590 --> 00:09:28,370
Jensen Huang is still the CEO,

279
00:09:28,370 --> 00:09:29,920
thirty-three years later.

280
00:09:29,930 --> 00:09:31,260
Still in the leather jacket.

281
00:09:31,780 --> 00:09:33,950
The kid who cleaned toilets in Kentucky

282
00:09:34,120 --> 00:09:37,313
